{hline}

{title:Title}

{p2colset 5 16 18 2}{...}
{p2col:{cmd:xtdhreg} {hline 2}}Random-effects double-hurdle regression{p_end}


{title:Syntax}

{p 8 15 2}
{cmd:xtdhreg}
{it:depvar}
{it:indepvars}
{ifin}
[{cmd:,} {it:options}]

{synoptset 20}{...}
{synopthdr}
{synoptline}
{synopt:{opt up}}choose upper instead of lower hurdle{p_end}
{synopt:{opt ptobit}}estimate only intercept of hurdle{p_end}
{synopt:{opt hd(varlist)}}specify {it:indepvars} for hurdle equation {p_end}
{synopt:{opt uncorr}}assume that error terms of both equations are uncorrelated{p_end}
{synopt:{opt trace}}display estimates for each iteration{p_end}
{synopt:{opt difficult}}use alternative algorithm for approximation{p_end}
{synopt:{opt constraints(numlist)}}define constraints{p_end}
{synoptline}
{p2colreset}{...}


{title:Description}

{pstd}
{cmd:xtdhreg} estimates random-effects double-hurdle regression.


{title:Options}

{phang}
{opt up} specifies that the upper, not the lower, limit of the support of the
dependent variable be treated as the hurdle.

{phang}
{opt ptobit} estimates the equation for the outer hurdle with just the
intercept.

{phang}
{opt hd(varlist)} allows a set of explanatory variables for the outer hurdle
that differs from the explanatory variables for the inner hurdle and those
realizations of the dependent variable that surmount the hurdle.

{phang}
{opt uncorr} assumes that the error terms of the hurdle equation and of the
main equation are uncorrelated.

{phang}
{opt trace} displays coefficients from each iteration.

{phang}
{opt difficult} uses an alternative, more calculation-intense algorithm for
approximation (which may help if the model does not converge).

{phang}
{opt constraints(numlist)} makes it possible for users to constrain the model.


{title:Remarks}

{pstd}
The model assumes a double hurdle.  Some observations are systematically
different and therefore categorically excluded from passing the hurdle.
Others might in principle pass.  But explanatory variables are such that they
nonetheless stay before the hurdle.

{pstd}
The estimator assumes a panel, that is, multiple observations from each
cross-section (usually an individual).  If you have not done so before, define
the panel using {cmd:xtset}.  Individuals who fall at the first hurdle must
never pass it.  Therefore, the model estimates an individual-specific error
term only conditional on the first hurdle being passed.  This is performed by
simulation using Halton draws.  For that purpose, the user-written program
{cmd:mdraws} is needed.  If it is not yet installed, please download it using
{cmd:findit mdraws}.

{pstd}
The program allows for arbitrary lower or upper limits.  Users should
therefore check for outliers (which otherwise would be treated as the hurdle).

{pstd}
Probit and random-effects tobit models from which starting values for maximum
likelihood estimation are derived are also displayed.  For the tobit component
of starting values, only individuals who ever pass the first hurdle are
considered.

{pstd}
To make sure the estimate of {bf:rho} is within [0,1], estimation works with
{bf:tan(rho)}, which is reported in the output as {cmd:transformed_rho}.  With
{cmd:nlcom}, this is transformed back to the actual correlation coefficient in
a separate line, and the confidence interval for this transformed estimate is
reported.

{pstd}
Using standard Wald tests, the program separately estimates and reports tests
for the joint significance of the explanatory variables used for
{cmd:equation(hurdle)}, {cmd:equation(above)}, or {cmd:equation(below)}.  An
overall test is performed to check whether both equations taken together
significantly explain the data.

{pstd}
The estimator is prepared to take factor variables, that is, variables in the
{cmd:i.}, {cmd:c.}, {cmd:#}, and {cmd:##} notation.  If such variables are
used, the Wald test for the joint significance of all coefficients must be
performed by hand.  See examples for syntax.  The {opt hd(varlist)} option
does not take factor variables.  If users want factors here, they must first
generate dummy variables.  If users want interaction terms or quadratic terms,
they must first generate auxiliary variables.  See example.

{pstd}
As a default, the program expects the user to specify a set of explanatory
variables for the hurdle equation (using the {opt hd()} option) that partly or
completely differ from the explanatory variables for those individuals who
pass the first hurdle.  If users want the complete set of explanatory
variables to be the same for both equations, they should repeat them in the
{opt hd()} option.

{pstd}
If {opt uncorr} is selected, the estimator assumes that the error terms of the
hurdle equation and of the equation conditional on the first hurdle being
passed are uncorrelated.  As a default, the correlation of both error terms is
estimated.

{pstd}
Stata's notation for variables and coefficients is available with

{phang2}
{cmd:. matrix list e(b)}{p_end}

{pstd}
Users can calculate marginal effects in postestimation using {cmd:margins}.
Stata treats the coefficients conditional on the first hurdle being passed as
{cmd:equation(above)} or as {cmd:equation(below)} if {opt up} is chosen.
Coefficients of the first hurdle are treated as {cmd:equation(hurdle)}.

{pstd}
The dependent variable should not have missing values.  When the auxiliary
variable for the first hurdle is generated, the missing values would be
treated as positive expressions.  Users should first create a dataset where
they drop these observations.

{pstd}
The estimator picks up the panel definition of the user.  It may therefore be
advisable to first use {cmd:xtset} and check whether the result is what the
user wants.

{pstd}
The estimator works with a series of auxiliary variables, all generated with
names preceded by an underscore, that is, {cmd:_i}.  At the end, the program
drops these variables.  However, this requires that the program not end
prematurely, that is, that you not use the {cmd:break} command.  If you do,
please type {cmd:discard} into the command line.  Then the program is
refreshed and first removes the old auxiliary variables.


{title:Examples}

{phang}{cmd:. xtdhreg y x, hd(z)}{p_end}
{phang}{cmd:. xtdhreg y x, ptobit}{p_end}
{phang}{cmd:. xtdhreg y x, hd(z) up uncorr trace}{p_end}
{phang}{cmd:. xtdhreg y c.x1##c.x2, hd(z)}{p_end}
{phang}{cmd:. margins, dydx(*) predict(equation(above))}{p_end}
{phang}{cmd:. test ([above] x1) ([above] x2) ([above] c.x1#c.x2)}{p_end}
{phang}{cmd:. xtdhreg y i.x1##c.x2, hd(z)}{p_end}
{phang}{cmd:. testparm i.x1 x2 i.x1#x2, equation(above)}{p_end}
{phang}{cmd:. generate intz1z2 = z1*z2}{p_end}
{phang}{cmd:. xtdhreg y x, hd(z1 z2 intz1z2)}{p_end}


{marker saved_results}{...}
{title:Stored results}

{pstd}
{cmd:dhreg} stores the following in {cmd:e()}:

{synoptset 20 tabbed}{...}
{p2col 5 20 24 2: Scalars}{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(N)}}number of observations{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(k)}}number of parameters{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(k_eq)}}number of equations in {cmd:e(b)}{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(k_eq_model)}}number of equations in overall model test{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(k_dv)}}number of dependent variables{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(df_m)}}model degrees of freedom{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(ll)}}log likelihood{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(chi2)}}chi-squared{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(p)}}significance{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(rank)}}rank of {cmd:e(V)}{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(ic)}}number of iterations{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(rc)}}return code{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(converged)}}{cmd:1} if converged, {cmd:0} otherwise{p_end}

{synoptset 20 tabbed}{...}
{p2col 5 20 24 2: Macros}{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(cmd)}}{cmd:xtdhreg}{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(depvar)}}name of dependent variable{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(chi2type)}}{cmd:Wald} or {cmd:LR}; type of model chi-squared test{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(vce)}}{it:vcetype} specified in {cmd:vce()}{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(opt)}}type of optimization{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(which)}}{cmd:max} or {cmd:min}; whether optimizer is to perform maximization or minimization{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(ml_method)}}type of {cmd:ml} method{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(user)}}name of likelihood-evaluator program{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(technique)}}maximization technique{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(properties)}}{cmd:b V}{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(predict)}}program used to implement {cmd:predict}{p_end}

{synoptset 20 tabbed}{...}
{p2col 5 20 24 2: Matrices}{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(b)}}coefficient vector{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(ilog)}}iteration log{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(gradient)}}gradient vector{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(V)}}variance-covariance matrix of the estimators{p_end}

{synoptset 20 tabbed}{...}
{p2col 5 20 24 2: Functions}{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:e(sample)}}marks estimation sample{p_end}
